Subject: [PATCH sched_ext/for-7.5 0/1] selftests/sched_ext: Catch unbound kfunc declarations in the scx headers
To: Tejun Heo <tj@kernel.org>
Cc: David Vernet <void@manifault.com>,
    Andrea Righi <arighi@nvidia.com>,
    Changwoo Min <changwoo@igalia.com>,
    Emil Tsalapatis <emil@etsalapatis.com>,
    fangqiurong <fangqiurong@kylinos.cn>,
    sched-ext@lists.linux.dev,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

A weak kfunc extern that libbpf can't bind stays NULL without an error,
so a wrong declaration in the scx headers makes its compat wrapper fall
back to an older kfunc on every kernel. Two of them shipped in v7.1 and
were fixed in v7.3 by 62f3d531e41a and 56bbc91219c0. Until then,
non-local reenq aborted the scheduler with "kernel too old", and
scx_bpf_dsq_move_to_local() dropped @enq_flags.

This adds compat_kfuncs. It runs a syscall program that records
bpf_ksym_exists() for each weak kfunc declaration in common.bpf.h and
for the newest variant of each kfunc in compat.bpf.h. It fails for a
declaration that didn't bind although the kernel has the function
libbpf looks it up as. It skips the ones whose function the kernel
doesn't have, e.g. the sub-sched kfuncs without CONFIG_EXT_SUB_SCHED.

Ihor's kfunc flavor series [1] would let the bare ___v2 spelling bind,
but not a declaration with the wrong arguments, like the one
62f3d531e41a fixed.

The list in compat_kfuncs.h needs the new variant whenever a compat
wrapper gets one. As compat.bpf.h is synced from the scx repo, I can
move the check there instead if you prefer.

Tested on for-7.5 (c6cc52a72055) in a 4-vCPU VM: compat_kfuncs passes
as is, and fails with either fix reverted.

Thanks.

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/r/20261117192153.3384031-1-ihor.solodrai@linux.dev/

Cheng-Yang Chou (1):
